(LST-57 — a map may be an element of a list: the slot carries the map tag,
 the list prints the map recursively, and an element extracted into a
 declared `map` variable reads its keys normally.
 On 0.4.9 and earlier, this did NOT work as the manual's old sentence
 (pre-0.4.9 line 2381-2383) implied — "a `For each` over such a list
 types the loop variable as a map". The loop variable carries the TAG
 (LST-55 proves `is a map` fires) but is not statically a map, so
 `entry's "tag"` inside the loop is a compile error. Discrepancy 2
 RESOLVED — the manual was corrected (LANGUAGE.md:2606-2620) to state
 exactly this rule and show the position-loop idiom below as the worked
 example, instead of the false "types the loop variable" claim. See
 Discrepancy 2, repro `D2.vox`.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox LST-57.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   [{"tag": 1}, {"tag": 2}]
   {"tag": 1}
   {"tag": 1}
   1
)
a list called holder is [{"tag": 1}, {"tag": 2}].
print holder.
print element 1 of holder.
a map called picked is element 1 of holder.
print picked.
print picked's "tag".
